  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Blue Tooth (Hydnellum caeruleum) is a striking woodland fungus characterized by its distinctive blue coloration on the cap and stem. This bolete relative belongs to the Boletopsidaceae family and is known for its tooth-like spines underneath rather than typical pores.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Blue Coloration",
      "description": "The cap and upper stem display a vibrant blue to blue-gray color, which is the most distinctive feature and gives the fungus its common name.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Tooth-like Spines",
      "description": "Instead of pores, the underside features decurrent teeth or spines that are white when young and may become pale gray with age.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Bruising Reaction",
      "description": "The flesh and spines often bruise or stain brown to blackish when handled or damaged, a helpful identification feature.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Blue Tooth typically grows in coniferous and mixed forests of New England, fruiting in late summer through fall on mossy ground and among leaf litter.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its appealing appearance, Hydnellum caeruleum is inedible and may cause gastrointestinal upset, making proper identification crucial for foragers.",
